          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a recurring neurological disorder. Headache is its most prominent feature, but it also often brings sensitivity to light and sound, and nausea. Most people have occasional attacks, while a minority have them chronically. The General clinical reference \"Migraine\" describes chronic migraine as headache on 15 or more days a month, with at least eight of those days showing migraine features, for more than three months. (Source: *Migraine*, General clinical reference.)\n\nA typical migraine headache is usually one-sided, pulsating, and moderate to severe. Routine physical activity makes it worse. Attacks often accompany nausea, vomiting, or sensitivity to light and sound. Untreated attacks commonly last from a few hours to three days. The same reference notes that not every attack has every feature, and that the headache is on both sides in a substantial share of people. That is one reason migraine is often mislabeled as an ordinary headache. (Source: *Migraine*, General clinical reference.)\n\nThe retrieved material did not explain in detail how migraine differs from other headache types, how it is diagnosed, or how it is treated. If you are wondering whether your own headaches fit this pattern, a doctor or other clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
